#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b10bf58-4bf8-54ec-afc7-ef5205e4325d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178270} {:answer/id #uuid "c14a5015-8a0d-5a0f-bd3d